plan Diagnostics
title   Actionable failures and safe diagnostics
status  proposed
goal
    App authors can identify a failed boundary, correct its cause and inspect all failures
    without parsing prose or leaking private data through diagnostics.
outcomes
    O1  FlowError and Diagnostic
        Expected invalid authoring throws FlowError. Owned Effects report understood expected
        failures as FlowError in the typed error channel, preserving their success and service
        requirement types. Defects and interruption remain distinct Effect outcomes.

        FlowError extends Error and exposes readonly _tag: "FlowError", code, diagnostics,
        optional cause and optional evidence. diagnostics is a nonempty readonly array;
        code equals diagnostics[0].code. The first diagnostic identifies the primary boundary
        failure; later diagnostics retain phase order and multiplicity.

        Diagnostic is an Error that authors can yield directly in Effect and encode/decode with
        its exported Schema. It exposes classification (Failure, Defect or Interruption), code,
        path (strings or finite numbers), details (string keys with string, finite number,
        boolean or null values), summary and help. Invalid tags, missing fields, unknown codes
        and values outside these domains reject. Authors branch on _tag, code, classification,
        path and details; summary and help may change without changing the failure's meaning.

        Schema rejection reports SchemaValidation with actual issue paths and safe details,
        unless the owning boundary defines a more specific code. Parser exceptions never escape
        as raw exceptions; arbitrary callback defects are never mislabeled SchemaValidation.
        Story failure evidence follows @plan.Testing.O8.
    O2  Recognizable codes and corrective action
        Codes identify failures independently of message wording. Each diagnostic names the
        affected field, identity or bound in path/details and gives a concrete corrective action
        in help. Reporting an issue does not itself mean the call rejects; the owning boundary
        decides whether work fails, continues or falls back. Application guard deny codes remain
        application data, separate from Flow's diagnostic vocabulary.

        Common codes an author can encounter:
        - SchemaValidation: correct the value at the reported Schema issue path.
        - InvalidEvent: use the receiving machine's public event and a valid payload.
        - RuntimeNotReady: await runtime.ready() before issuing the command.
        - RuntimeDisposed: create a new Runtime for the current scope.
        - AdmissionOverloaded: wait for accepted work or reduce the submitted batch.
        - StorageFailure: restore storage availability or quota; do not assume the write is durable.
        - SecondTabNoPersistence: close the other persistence owner or continue without persistence.
        - HydrationScopeMismatch: use the same scope for the payload and receiving provider.
        - CleanupFailed: repair the finalizer and replace the owning Runtime.
        - Defect: fix the failing callback or service using the in-process cause.
        - Interruption: honor cleanup; retry only while the original intent still holds.

        These examples are not an exhaustive code catalog. Boundary-specific behavior belongs to
        its owner: machine validation @plan.Machines.O1; event validation @plan.Machines.O3;
        readiness @plan.Runtime.O2; identity @plan.Runtime.O3; overload @plan.Runtime.O4;
        disposal @plan.Runtime.O6; operation inputs @plan.Operations.O1; retry @plan.Operations.O3;
        storage failures @plan.Persistence.O5; second-tab fallback @plan.Persistence.O7;
        hydration @plan.ServerRendering.O4 and scope isolation @plan.ServerRendering.O6;
        lazy loading @plan.LazyFeatures.O5; Story processing @plan.Testing.O2;
        trace validation @plan.Inspection.O4; CLI usage @plan.CommandLine.O1.
    O3  Complete failures survive cleanup
        An author inspecting an in-process failure can recover its complete Effect Cause when
        available, including failures from finalizers and cancellation. Cleanup never replaces
        the execution failure or erases another failure. Classification is Defect if any defect
        exists, otherwise Failure if a typed failure exists, otherwise Interruption.

        Classification does not reorder diagnostics: execution or cancellation stays primary;
        when only cleanup fails, the first cleanup failure is primary. Remaining cleanup
        diagnostics retain phase order and multiplicity, including after end capture.
        Operation failure, defect, interruption and an unknown remote outcome remain distinct.
        Promise adapters and forwarded AbortSignal cancellation preserve the complete Cause
        until the host deliberately projects the result.

        Only the in-process FlowError may expose raw Cause. Cause, stack traces and fiber ids
        never enter snapshots, checkpoints, persistence, SSR payloads, traces, inspector events
        or CLI output. Outbound application data follows @plan.Inspection.O5; SSR omission
        follows @plan.ServerRendering.O3.
    O4  Safe rendering
        Rendering a Diagnostic preserves its code, path, safe details and actionable help.
        Control characters in paths, details, summary and help are escaped so hostile names
        cannot inject terminal or log commands. Output never exposes raw input, stacks or Cause,
        and never invents a file, line or column. Locations appear only when actually known.
decisions
    D1  One public failure contract
        FlowError is the shared boundary carrier and Diagnostic its shared diagnostic Schema.
        Capability-specific error classes or competing diagnostic codecs are not public APIs.
verification
    V1  Codec and carrier tests prove @O1's fields, rejection rules and primary code.
    V2  Type tests prove readonly diagnostics, yieldable Diagnostic and preserved Effect A/R.
    V3  Boundary tests prove expected failures, Schema paths and distinct callback defects.
    V4  Mixed-Cause tests prove complete causes, classification and ordering through cleanup.
    V5  Rendering/export tests prove safe actionable output without leaks or invented locations.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        Complete boundary-code catalog and per-code remediation wording.
        Closed reason vocabulary and diagnostic codec implementation.
        Host execution helpers and printer organization.
        Exact evidence projections and counters within the owning evidence contract.
